{"type": "thinking", "text": "Oops that scrolled the list, not settings. I'm in My Tasks. Let me get back to settings > Task list options and scroll to bottom.\n\nActually, let me think about the subtask order test directly and what \"global direction setting\" might be. In Tasks.org source (I have some familiarity):\n\nThere's `SettingsFragment` → \"Task list options\" contains:\n- Font size\n- Row spacing\n- Show full task title\n- Show description\n- Show full description\n- Show links\n- Per-list sorting\n- Chips (chip appearance, subtasks, start date, places, lists, tags)\n\nHmm, no direction.\n\n\"Date and time\" has:\n- Calendar\n- Time zone maybe\n\n\"Task defaults\":\n- New tasks on top\n- Default list\n- Default tags\n- Default priority\n- Default start date\n- Default due date\n- Default calendar\n- Default reminders\n\n\"New tasks on top\" is the closest to a \"global direction setting\": it determines whether new tasks are added to the top (direction). Maybe the change: nested subtasks in manual mode now follow the global direction setting = whether new tasks go on top or bottom (Settings > Task defaults > New tasks on top). Hmm, but \"nested items ... follow the global direction setting\" likely means the *sort direction* (ascending/descending) selected in the Sort sheet.\n\nLet me test the actual observable: Set group to \"None\", Sorting to \"By due date Ascending\" vs Descending; Subtasks \"My order\". Compare subtask order under Pack for trip. But subtasks have no due dates, so irrelevant.\n\nHmm... Actually maybe \"nested items\" refers to nested task list rows in *manual mode* = when Sorting = My order. In manual mode, the order of tasks is manual, and nested subtasks appear under parent. \"follow the global direction setting\" might mean: when the global sort direction is Descending, the manual order is reversed. I recall Tasks.org has a setting \"Reverse manual sort order\" or something in the Sort sheet when \"My order\" is selected? Hmm.\n\nWait! Maybe the Sort sheet's \"Subtasks: My order\" option has a direction toggle when \"My order\" is the *Sorting* (not Subtasks)? On Default list I saw Sorting My order with no direction. Hmm.\n\nActually, I wonder if \"global direction\" = the \"Grouping Ascending/Descending\" or \"Sorting Ascending/Descending\" setting that also applies to subtask nesting. To test, I could:\n1. Set Subtasks = My order, Sorting = By title Descending, Grouping = By due date.\n2. Compare subtasks order: currently Print tickets then Charge phone.\n3. Change direction to Ascending; check if subtask order flips to Charge phone then Print tickets? That would be \"nested items following the global direction setting\". If the behavior is reversed (i.e., it flips when it shouldn't or doesn't flip when it should), that's the seeded bug.\n\nBut what is the *correct* expected behavior? The statement says nested items in manual mode now follow the global direction setting. So with global direction Descending, manual subtasks should display in reverse manual order. The \"manual order\" for Pack for trip is presumably Print tickets then Charge phone (the order seeded). Hmm, but which is the manual order? If seeded as Print tickets, Charge phone, then Descending should show Charge phone, Print tickets.\n\nHowever, \"Subtasks: My order\" may use the *child task's* `position` or list ordering. Hmm.\n\nI think I should experiment. First, restore sorting to something (currently By due date Ascending, grouping None, because I changed Default list settings globally? Wait, that changed My Tasks too: My Tasks shows grouping None, sorting By due date).\n\nLet me set Grouping back to \"By due date\" and Sorting \"By title\" Ascending (as original) to have a clean state, then test subtask direction by toggling.\n\nActually the original state was: Grouping By due date Ascending, Sorting By due date Ascending, Subtasks My order. I've changed: Sorting -> By title Asc -> Desc; Default list Sorting -> My order (but per-list? seems Default list kept My order); then Default list Sorting By due date while grouping None; My Tasks now grouping None, sorting By due date.\n\nHmm, the state is messy. Let me restore: My Tasks sort sheet: Grouping By due date Ascending; Sorting By due date Ascending; Subtasks My order. Then Default list also should be By due date (it shares global since per-list off?). Wait, but Default list retained \"My order\" while My Tasks showed \"By title Descending\", which indicates per-list preferences ARE being used despite \"Per-list sorting\" being off? Hmm! That's suspicious and may be the actual seeded bug in the changed area: sort sheet options resolved from the current list's preferences.\n\nLet me re-examine: When I selected Sorting \"My order\" in the Default list sheet, the sheet changed grouping to None and Sorting My order. Then I went to My Tasks; its sheet showed Grouping None, Sorting By title Descending. Then back to Default list sheet showed Grouping None, Sorting My order. So each list maintained its own sorting! But \"Per-list sorting\" setting is OFF, which should mean they share global settings. Unless... per-list sorting being off means... hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the \"Per-list sorting\" toggle we saw was actually ON? In Task list options, [16] \"Per-list sorting\" and [17] View [clickable] — no \"checked\" marker. In this observe format, checked toggles show \"checked\". So it's off. Hmm.\n\nBut wait, maybe I'm misreading: The Task list options screen's [16]/[17] was \"Per-list sorting ... Remember sort and group settings for each list independently\" with View [clickable] (not checked) — so off.\n\nYet the lists show different sort settings. That contradicts \"Per-list sorting off\". Could be a seeded bug in the sort sheet: it resolves options from the *current list's stored preference* even when per-list sorting is disabled, causing lists to diverge. Hmm, but the *list rendering* also diverged? We observed My Tasks and Default list each with their own order? Default list empty so can't compare order. Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually maybe when per-list sorting is OFF, the app uses a single global preference. The sort sheet change \"options are now resolved from the current list's preferences\" might mean the sheet displays per-list prefs regardless. Then when I set My order on Default list, it enabled grouping None globally too (we saw My Tasks Grouping None). But Sorting didn't propagate (My Tasks showed By title Descending). So partial propagation! That's a bug: the sort sheet options in one list partially apply to another. Hmm.\n\nLet me carefully test the cross-list behavior:\n1. Ensure Per-list sorting OFF (currently).\n2. Set My Tasks: Grouping By due date Ascending, Sorting By title Ascending.\n3. Go to Default list: check sheet shows same Grouping By due date Ascending, Sorting By title Ascending.\n4. Change Default list Sorting to By priority Descending.\n5. Go to My Tasks: check sheet and order: should reflect By priority Descending if global.\n\nIf not shared, that's a bug (when per-list sorting off, settings should be shared globally). But is that the intended behavior? The setting description: \"Per-list sorting: Remember sort and group settings for each list independently\". Off => don't remember per list => share. So yes, cross-list divergence is a bug.\n\nBut wait — maybe if \"Per-list sorting\" is OFF, the app uses the *last used* sort globally, and my observations were consistent: after setting Default list My order, My Tasks grouping was None (changed) but sorting By title (unchanged)? Under a single global pref, both should change. Unless changing Sorting to My order on Default list failed to persist globally but grouping None did? Hmm.\n\nActually maybe what happened: Selecting \"My order\" on Default list set global grouping None, global sorting My order. Then when I opened My Tasks, since My order is not available for My Tasks, the app reset Sorting to the previous non-manual (