ode follow the global direction setting\" if that's the meaning. But adding a subtask changes data (I can delete it after).\n\nHmm, but first I should figure out what direction means. Let me try selecting Subtasks sort = \"By due date Descending\" and see if there's a direction option. Actually the Subtasks submenu had no direction options (My order, By due date, etc.), so direction for subtasks likely comes from the main Sorting row's direction! I.e., \"Subtasks: By due date\" uses the global direction (Ascending/Descending). For \"My order\", direction may also follow the global Sorting direction now (the change!). But our test showed no flip. Hmm — unless my expectation of the manual order is wrong: maybe the manual order is Charge phone then Print tickets, and with Sorting Descending it displays reversed as Print tickets, Charge phone. And originally (before I toggled) with Ascending it also displayed Print tickets first... wait, originally Sorting was By due date Ascending with Subtasks My order; displayed Print tickets, Charge phone. If manual order were Charge phone, Print tickets, then Ascending should show Charge phone first. So no.\n\nHmm, wait. Did the list re-render after I changed Sorting direction? I closed the sheet and observed; the subtask order remained Print tickets, Charge phone. The main tasks within groups: with Descending, the \"Due Monday\" group should show N/A since only header visible at bottom. We saw the top groups: Buy milk group, Pack for trip group - single items, no change visible.\n\nLet me verify the global sort direction actually took effect: the \"Due Monday, December 14\" group at bottom; scroll to see order of Water plants/Final dose/Weekly review. With Sorting By due date Descending, they'd be ordered descending by due date: all Dec 14; the secondary order might be reversed. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the seeded bug is that the subtask order does NOT follow the global direction (should flip but doesn't). To be more certain, I could compare