{"type": "thinking", "text": "Read docs: title, no due date, no repeat, description \"See https://example.com/guide\". All correct. Good.\n\nNow test \"Show links\" ON to see if links are rendered? Not necessary; but maybe the seeded bug is about showing links in the list. The precondition explicitly mentions the link. Hmm. Let me check the list display: \"See https://example.com/guide\" plain. If \"Show links\" is off, it's expected plain text with no rich link. Fine.\n\nLet me test toggling \"Show links\" ON (from Task list options) and see if the list renders the link with rich formatting; then toggle back. This checks consistency. But it's a settings behavior, not a changed area. Might be a rabbit hole; skip.\n\nNow, the \"sort sheet resolved from current list's preferences\" — I tested per-list ON with two lists having different sort and each sheet showed its own. That's the changed functionality working. But wait, I should test the sheet *after a change* in the current list, i.e., does the sheet reflect the list's preferences after restart? We did: My Tasks By title Asc persisted. Good.\n\nHmm, but maybe there's a more subtle seeded bug: the sort sheet options might be resolved from the *wrong* list (e.g., the previously viewed list) when opening immediately after switching lists. Let me test: switch from Default list (By due date Asc) to My Tasks (should show My Tasks' prefs). Earlier when per-list was off (global), both shared... when per-list was ON, after switching from Default list to My Tasks, the My Tasks sheet showed By title Asc (its own). That worked.\n\nOK, so sort sheet seems fine.\n\nNow the remaining suspicious area: subtask nesting direction. Let me look more carefully. Maybe the changed feature is about **nested item ordering following \"Sorting\" direction** when Subtasks=My order; we observed no flip. But maybe I should verify with a task whose subtasks have due dates? No, subtasks have no dates.\n\nAlternative: maybe \"nested items in manual mode\" refers to the **subtask rows' placement relative to the parent when the parent is sorted**: e.g., when Sorting is Descending, the subtasks should appear *above* their parent (because descending nests them above)? Let's check: In the earlier test with Sorting Descending/Grouping Asc (or Desc), the subtasks still appeared *below* the parent. Hmm, maybe the feature is: in manual mode with Descending, nested items appear above the parent. We didn't test manual mode (Sorting My order) on a list with tasks. Default list is empty.\n\nHmm! We can test manual mode on Default list by creating a temporary task with a subtask there. Data churn but reversible. Steps:\n1. In Default list view, create task \"QA parent\" with a subtask \"QA child 1\" and maybe \"QA child 2\". Actually creating subtask via the editor after saving parent.\n2. Set Sorting = My order on Default list (which forces Grouping None).\n3. Observe nested order under QA parent.\n4. Toggle the global direction? In manual mode, the Sorting row has no direction; but maybe Grouping direction? Not present (None). Hmm. So the \"global direction setting\" for manual mode must be elsewhere... maybe Settings > Task defaults > New tasks on top. If I toggle that and the nested order flips, we have our answer.\n\nActually, wait! Maybe with per-list sorting ON/OFF, the \"global direction\" comes from the *global* sort settings even when the list uses My order. E.g., Settings global \"Sorting: By due date Ascending\" direction might be the \"global direction\" applied to manual subtasks. Hmm.\n\nI've spent a lot; maybe I should settle on reporting the observable inconsistency:\n- In the task list with Subtasks = My order, the nested items Print tickets and Charge phone display in reverse of the order shown in the Pack for trip task editor (Charge phone, Print tickets).\nThat's a clear inconsistency between two screens, and it involves subtask nesting in the task list. The \"expected\" per the change note: nested items in manual mode should follow the global direction setting; with the global direction Ascending, they should appear in the task's manual order (Charge phone then Print tickets). Observed: Print tickets first (reverse). This is likely the seeded bug!\n\nBut wait: is the editor order actually \"manual order\"? In the editor, the subtasks are listed Charge phone then Print tickets. The sort sheet \"Subtasks: My order\" means manual order. If the list is in My order mode, it should match the editor's order (manual). It doesn't. So the bug is clear regardless of direction semantics: two screens disagree on manual order. Good — I can report this as a consistency bug: \"My order\" nested items are displayed reversed relative to the task's own subtask order.\n\nHmm, but could the editor sort by a different method? The editor's subtask rows might be ordered by \"position\" (manual), while the list sorts by position too but *descending* because of the global direction. The changed feature says nested items in manual mode should follow the global direction setting — if the global direction were Descending, then the list's reversal is *correct* and the editor is just showing manual order (not direction-dependent). Hmm! So the list being reversed might be intended if the global direction is descending. But the sort sheet shows Ascending... The \"global direction setting\" might be the *main Sorting direction*, which is Ascending. So reversal is wrong.\n\nUnless the \"global direction setting\" is a separate global preference not exposed in the sort sheet (maybe stored as \"sort direction = DESCENDING\" globally while the visible per-list sort is ascending). Hmm.\n\nLet me try to find a \"global direction setting\" once more by checking \"Settings > Task list options\" bottom for a \"Reverse\" toggle. Earlier list: Font size, Row spacing, Show full task title, Show description, Show full description, Show links, Per-list sorting, Chips header, Chip appearance, Subtasks, Start date, Places, Lists, Tags. Maybe below Tags there's more. Let me scroll to the very bottom."}
